hi guys it's me quinn and as always if  you appreciate my content consider  hitting the like button it's the only  way the youtube algorithm really notices  me  galactic civilizations have long been a  staple of science fiction  humankind's transition from the egg that  is the earth into the realm of space  just seems like a natural evolution  as civilization grows more resources are  required  space has infinite resources and the  earth itself is fragile  if something catastrophic ever happened  all of humankind would be put at risk  spreading into the reaches of space  would prevent this  a galactic civilization not only seems  like a logical step  it's perhaps a necessary one  still as they are depicted in science  fiction galactic empires do indeed seem  to possess a number of key problems  the first problem is communication  if humanity is spread throughout space  on different planets then conventional  methods of communication would not be  practical  inhabitable planets could be light years  away from one another without the  ability to communicate efficiently no  form of galactic government could exist  and human cosmic society would be no  more than a collection of planets  operating mainly independent of each  other the solution to such a problem  would of course be technological  innovation which is the approach of most  science fiction  in the dune saga instantaneous  communication is possible through utes  of the holtzman effect the holtzman  effect is a fictional scientific  phenomenon discovered by abraham von  holtzmann according to the dune  encyclopedia it allowed for  instantaneous communication between  distant worlds similarly in dan simmons  hyperion cantos communication between  worlds is possible through use of fat  line technology the worlds had to be  previously set up with fatline  technology infrastructure beforehand  worlds connected in this method also had  the capability to allow for access to  the all thing essentially an advanced  form of the internet  fatline technology had been gifted to  humanity by the techno core and only  they had an intimate understanding of  how exactly the technology worked  sixteen law's remembrance of earth's  past trilogy  solves the problem of communication with  technology involving the phenomenon of  quantum entanglement  this method unlike the one appearing in  dune hyperion and in most other science  fiction series is actually based upon  real scientific principles  quantum entanglement is also sometimes  referred to as the quantum non-local  connection is a property of a quantum  mechanical system  in which two particles can be linked  together in such a way that one cannot  be described without full mention of its  counterpart this is true regardless of  the particle's physical distance from  each other in the universe  the next problem is commerce and travel  similarly to the problem of  communication commerce and travel would  be necessary for a galactic society to  function it's practical to assume that  some worlds would possess resources that  others did not some would be  agriculturally oriented some would be  worlds rich in natural resources and  precious metals  if trade between worlds is not possible  then there really is no cosmic  civilization at all for trade is a  fundamental aspect of all civilization  if humans cannot travel between worlds  in a reasonable amount of time it would  be near impossible for any kind of  interplanetary culture to develop  in the dune universe the time before the  guild whose navigators could navigate  the vacuum of space was a dark age after  the fall of the machines mankind could  no longer move safely throughout space  as they could not avoid obstacles  this made space travel often deadly the  emergence of the guild was so  significant in the revitalization of the  declining empire  that their calendar was reset after its  formation  the time before the guild was given the  epoch bg  before guilt and ag  after guilt  beyond this without the ability to move  freely throughout the universe a  civilization becomes vulnerable in other  ways  sixth and low's remembrance of earth's  trilogy depicts the human race's  struggle to acquire faster than light  travel  in the books you see the helplessness of  a civilization without the ability to  move efficiently throughout the cosmos  in a universe potentially populated with  billions of other civilizations the  danger extends greatly without the  ability to escape enemies and natural  disasters the species is a sitting duck  among piranhas  in some series such as hyperion there  are certain issues associated with  specific kinds of space travel  in normal space travel relativity has to  be considered  this all of course relates back to  einstein's famous equation e equals m c  squared  time moves slower for those traveling  through the vacuum of space  at near light speed than for those  stationary on planets  a traveler may find that after returning  from a voyage off world that their loved  ones have died or aged beyond  recognition  even if you could solve these problems  and actually manage to get a galactic  society functioning  then comes the question of leadership  regulation and bureaucracy  and all of these have various issues  that can be associated with them just  stretched out to a galactic scale  on earth human civilizations often  gravitated to a model of centralized  leadership  organized in pyramid fashion with the  most valuable and most powerful at the  top  a major problem however arises when this  style of governance is applied to  galactic society  the more a cosmic civilization grows the  harder it becomes for centralized  government to handle its bureaucratic  and regulatory demands  in asimov's foundation series this is  the key cause for the fall of the empire  in the series the capital world tran  tour had long been the center of  imperial administration  the demands of such a large empire  proved to be too much for a centralized  style of leadership  the collapse of the entire system began  with trantor and when it fell the empire  fell  in a galactic empire led this way some  worlds and people would inevitably be  neglected  and because certain worlds would be  given more attention than others this  would lead to problems of inequality  throughout the inhabited universe  this would ultimately weaken the unity  of any galactic empire as humankind's  baseline sense of inherent equality  would be destroyed  because of this resentment towards the  centralized ruling class would no doubt  increase  this would lead to rebellions inevitably  ensuing  these rebellions would also threaten the  stability of the empire on a galactic  scale these problems compound upon each  other until they are unmanageable  leaders incapable of managing the  problems arising from a growing empire  then often turn to cruel methods to  suppress rebels and to quell any  potential unrest in the future in turn  more often than not this simply makes  things worse increasing the resentment  towards the ruling class and it becomes  a vicious cycle that erodes the  integrity of the empire as a whole  frank herbert's dune saga also makes an  argument directly in opposition to the  idea of centralized leadership on a  galactic scale the main idea being if  one person could rule over all of  humanity then one threat could  annihilate it this highlights the  inherent threat of centralized  leadership on the cosmic stage the  magnifying effect that power and  influence has on the actions of certain  individuals is amplified  instead of an individual's actions  potentially affecting an entire country  the whole of humanity is affected  this was the effect with trantor in  asimov's series and the same principle  is also demonstrated in sixteen lewis  series with the concept of the wall  facers and later the sword holders  of course there are always workarounds  to these problems and many science  fiction series have shown examples still  often much of the conflict and driving  narratives of many science fiction  series are tied to the inherent problems  which arise when humanity attempts to  exist on a galactic scale and add that  to the fact that sci-fi empires are  often governed in an archaic manner  why does there need to be an emperor we  outgrew emperors on earth i hope why  would we have them in the future that's  going backwards  a functioning galactic society likely  wouldn't resemble any society on earth  because a new system of governance would  have to form in order to accommodate the  current state of human society  this video was of course brought to you  by my wonderful patrons if you would  like to support this channel please  click the patreon link in the  description  [Music]  foreign
 

